WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.849 --> 00:00:05.180
Although the Losers were able to send Pennywise
the Clown running at the tail end of It, there's

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:09.890
still a whole 'nother round of demon-fighting
yet to come when Chapter Two hits theaters.

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:13.510
The film will revisit the group as they're
called back to Derry, Maine to fulfill their

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:17.320
oath to battle It again, nearly three decades
later.

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:21.860
Director Andy Muschietti is expected to return
for the sequel alongside screenwriter Gary

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:24.990
Dauberman, and the film's production is scheduled
to kick off sometime this summer.

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:25.990
Here's what else we know about It: Chapter
Two so far.

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:27.230
Beware of spoilers.

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:28.699
Calling back the kids

00:00:28.699 --> 00:00:32.140
Although the septet of kids who carried the
first film fulfilled their duties to take

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:36.109
down the clown, the young actors portraying
them are still expected to reprise their roles

00:00:36.109 --> 00:00:38.210
for at least part of the sequel.

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:42.179
Just as Stephen King's novel bounced back
between the adult and children's perspectives,

00:00:42.179 --> 00:00:45.920
Chapter Two will feature some memory lane
journeying that'll show us some more of what

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:47.179
the kids went through.

00:00:47.179 --> 00:00:49.510
As Muschietti explained to Entertainment Weekly:

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:53.219
"On the second movie, that dialogue between
timelines will be more present.

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:56.789
If we're telling the story of adults, we are
going to have flashbacks that take us back

00:00:56.789 --> 00:01:00.030
to the '80s and inform the story in the present
day."

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:04.190
Muschietti added that the kids will be "a
big part of the action," and since Bill Skarsgard

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:09.140
himself will return to reprise Pennywise,
things are bound to be just as scary for everyone

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:10.660
involved this time around, too.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:13.000
A new timeline

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.980
Instead of having the kids do battle in Derry
during the '50s, as seen in Stephen King's

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:22.130
story and the 1990 TV mini-series adaptation,
the cinematic version of It takes places in

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:23.500
the late '80s.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:27.579
That means that the new film will take place
in present day, since the child-slaying monster

00:01:27.579 --> 00:01:30.259
returns to feed every 27 years.

00:01:30.259 --> 00:01:33.830
While it might seem like something will be
lost in the way of nostalgia without the second

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:38.320
feature taking place in a throwback period,
that's actually the way King originally intended

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:42.509
it, since he also set the adult-era events
of his story in the same time period of the

00:01:42.509 --> 00:01:43.509
book's release.

00:01:43.509 --> 00:01:47.880
We'll just have to wait and see whether the
era of smartphones, 24-hour media coverage,

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:51.960
and GPS tracking have an impact on the events
of the story, of course.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:56.090
But considering It is an eternal kind of evil,
this slight change in setting shouldn't make

00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:57.749
too much of a difference.

00:01:57.749 --> 00:01:58.880
Courting a cast

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:03.219
The cast of the first It movie have already
come up with a few ideas as to who should

00:02:03.219 --> 00:02:05.649
play their grown-up characters in the second
film.

00:02:05.649 --> 00:02:10.550
Finn Wolfhard sees Bill Hader being a good
match for the next Richie Tozier, while Wyatt

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:15.240
Oleff said Joseph Gordon-Levitt might be a
good match for Stanley Uris, and Jaeden Lieberher

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:19.500
sees Christian Bale as his adult counterpart
of choice for Bill Denbrough.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:23.970
Meanwhile, Chosen Jacobs pictures the Black
Panther himself, Chadwick Boseman, as the

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:25.860
grown-up version of Mike Hanlon.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:29.910
And he's not the only one with Marvel-ous
ambitions: Jeremy Ray Taylor is eyeing the

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:34.820
Star-Lord, Chris Pratt take over as the newly-buffed
up Ben Hanscom in the new movie.

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:39.220
Meanwhile, Jack Dylan Grazer would fancast
Jake Gyllenhaal as Eddie Kaspbrak.

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:44.610
Last but not least, Sophia Lillis likes Jessica
Chastain for Beverly Marsh 2.0.

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:48.050
They're all solid suggestions, and at least
one of them may come true.

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:52.290
Variety reports that Chastain has officially
entered talks to star as Beverly Marsh in

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:56.770
Chapter Two, which is probably not too surprising
since she and Muschietti previously worked

00:02:56.770 --> 00:03:02.050
together on film Mama and have both expressed
an interest in re-teaming for this project.

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:03.050
Other dimensions

00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:07.970
Sure, the first It film included some odd
moments, like when Pennywise broke out a strange

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:10.880
dance move and still managed to be terrifying.

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:15.050
But things could have been a lot weirder if
the source material is anything to go by.

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:19.520
In Stephen King's original text, the horror
sprinkles in some high-concept sci-fi as it

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:25.000
explores the cosmic origins of Pennywise and
a giant turtle god called Maturin, which basically

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:26.900
vomited up the universe.

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:31.100
Nods to the turtle were spotted by eagle-eyed
fans in the first film, but the multiverse,

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:35.260
and Pennywise's other forms, were largely
ignored all the same.

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:38.920
Muschietti has since pointed to the film's
limited budget and scope as reasons for not

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:43.320
featuring these scenes in the film but plans
to bring them into the picture for part two.

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:46.580
That means we're going to find out a whole
lot more about the deadlights and venture

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:50.210
forth into new realms of existence to fight
this evil.

00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:52.510
A grim return

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:56.590
As King's readers and fans of the mini-series
will know, Stanley Uris does not take the

00:03:56.590 --> 00:03:58.420
news of It's return very well.

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:01.800
And he's not the only one who's going to struggle
with emotional distress in the wake of what's

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:04.310
happened to him and the other kids of his
hometown.

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:08.120
Mike Hanlon, too, will endure some personal
struggles as a result of their encounter with

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:09.120
It.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:13.930
By the time the others return to their hometown,
Hanlon will have turned to drugs in his despair.

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:17.750
Muschietti describes the character as a "junkie"
in the sequel, but it turns out that his addiction

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:21.019
also serves a vital purpose in the fight against
Pennywise.

00:04:21.019 --> 00:04:25.720
In the book, younger versions of the Losers
Club deliberately inhale fumes in a "smoke-hole"

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:30.610
to learn more about their foe, but here, Hanlon's
experience with drugs as an adult will alter

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:34.890
his mind and help him to figure out how to
defeat the ancient creature via the bizarrely

00:04:34.890 --> 00:04:37.080
named "Ritual of Chüd."

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:41.220
While some fans of the book may object to
this change, Muschietti claims the idea is

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:46.020
to infuse Hanlon's character with "more agency,"
providing him with a more important role during

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:50.540
the gap where the rest of the Losers Club
found success in their lives away from Derry.

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:52.410
Deleted scenes float

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:57.270
The theatrical version of It was well over
two hours long, and it still didn't represent

00:04:57.270 --> 00:04:59.480
Muschietti's full vision of the story.

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:03.500
The director's producing partner sister Barbara
Muschietti hinted that a scene with white

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:07.520
supremacists burning down the Black Spot nightclub
could still appear in the sequel.

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:11.620
Meanwhile, Muschietti revealed he'd wanted
to include an ancient flashback to It's first

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:16.710
encounter with human beings and a scene that
showed Bill happening upon a bunch of balloons

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:18.760
and fallen children in the stream.

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:23.410
Perhaps most grisly of all, an early draft
of the first film's script included a colonial

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:28.430
England scene wherein Pennywise forced a mother
to give up her own baby for It to devour.

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:32.790
If Muschietti and his team are turning to
the cutting room floor for inspiration, and

00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:36.639
any or all of these scenes are folded into
Chapter Two, well, we're getting a pretty

00:05:36.639 --> 00:05:40.450
good taste of just how terrifying the second
It film will be.

00:05:40.450 --> 00:05:45.800
It: Chapter Two is slated to hit theaters
on September 6th, 2019.

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:47.950
"Time to float."

00:05:47.950 --> 00:05:48.990
Thanks for watching!

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:51.700
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:54.530
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

